1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:00,721
One prompt.

2
00:00:00,721 --> 00:00:01,835
Five hidden jobs.

3
00:00:01,835 --> 00:00:03,800
And an answer you can't check.

4
00:00:03,800 --> 00:00:08,976
That's what happens when you ask Claude to
analyse a quarter of market data and

5
00:00:08,976 --> 00:00:11,204
propose strategies, all in one go.

6
00:00:11,804 --> 00:00:13,364
Here's where we're heading:

7
00:00:13,364 --> 00:00:16,657
the same request as five small
steps, each one checkable.

8
00:00:16,657 --> 00:00:21,394
By the end of this lesson, you'll be able to
break any big business request into a

9
00:00:21,394 --> 00:00:25,033
chain of steps that Claude gets
right, and that you can verify.

10
00:00:25,633 --> 00:00:26,580
Chapter one.

11
00:00:26,580 --> 00:00:28,237
Spot the hidden jobs.

12
00:00:28,837 --> 00:00:31,239
Here's a prompt many of us have typed:

13
00:00:31,239 --> 00:00:34,715
analyse our Q3 market data and
propose five strategies.

14
00:00:34,715 --> 00:00:38,444
It looks like one request, but it
hides at least five jobs:

15
00:00:38,444 --> 00:00:43,626
read the data, spot the trends, explain the
causes, generate ideas, and rank them.

16
00:00:43,626 --> 00:00:47,734
Asked in one breath, the model has to
juggle all of them at once.

17
00:00:47,734 --> 00:00:53,043
The usual result is generic strategies, skipped
steps, or numbers that were never in

18
00:00:53,043 --> 00:00:53,612
the data.

19
00:00:54,212 --> 00:00:59,032
Chain of thought means asking the model to
write out its reasoning before it gives

20
00:00:59,032 --> 00:00:59,679
the answer.

21
00:00:59,679 --> 00:01:04,617
Each step is on the page, so later steps can
build on earlier ones, and you can read

22
00:01:04,617 --> 00:01:07,497
the reasoning to see exactly
where it went wrong.

23
00:01:07,497 --> 00:01:12,259
Same model, same data, but more of the work
happens out loud, which usually means

24
00:01:12,259 --> 00:01:14,375
fewer errors on multi-step problems.

25
00:01:14,975 --> 00:01:17,374
Task decomposition works one level up.

26
00:01:17,374 --> 00:01:22,488
Instead of one prompt doing everything, you
break the project into small, ordered

27
00:01:22,488 --> 00:01:23,119
sub-tasks.

28
00:01:23,119 --> 00:01:26,970
Each one has a clear input, a
single job, and a clear output.

29
00:01:26,970 --> 00:01:30,379
Chain of thought improves the
reasoning inside a step.

30
00:01:30,379 --> 00:01:33,599
Decomposition improves the
structure between steps.

31
00:01:33,599 --> 00:01:34,672
You'll want both.

32
00:01:35,272 --> 00:01:35,901
Takeaway:

33
00:01:35,901 --> 00:01:40,095
if a request hides several jobs,
give each job its own step.

34
00:01:40,695 --> 00:01:41,605
Chapter two.

35
00:01:41,605 --> 00:01:43,576
Turn one prompt into five.

36
00:01:44,176 --> 00:01:45,826
Here's the before version:

37
00:01:45,826 --> 00:01:50,204
one prompt, the Q3 data pasted in, and
a request for five strategies.

38
00:01:50,204 --> 00:01:51,536
You do get an answer.

39
00:01:51,536 --> 00:01:56,802
But you can't tell which numbers came from the
data, why these five were chosen, or

40
00:01:56,802 --> 00:01:58,452
where the reasoning broke.

41
00:01:59,052 --> 00:02:00,633
Now the after version.

42
00:02:00,633 --> 00:02:01,280
Step one:

43
00:02:01,280 --> 00:02:04,946
extract the key metrics, and
quote the source rows.

44
00:02:04,946 --> 00:02:05,593
Step two:

45
00:02:05,593 --> 00:02:07,750
find the three biggest trends.

46
00:02:07,750 --> 00:02:08,540
Step three:

47
00:02:08,540 --> 00:02:11,344
explain the likely cause of each trend.

48
00:02:11,344 --> 00:02:12,062
Step four:

49
00:02:12,062 --> 00:02:15,872
brainstorm ten strategy options
tied to those causes.

50
00:02:15,872 --> 00:02:16,591
Step five:

51
00:02:16,591 --> 00:02:20,616
score them on impact and effort,
and pick the best five.

52
00:02:20,616 --> 00:02:23,707
Every output becomes the
next step's input.

53
00:02:24,307 --> 00:02:25,371
Phrasing matters.

54
00:02:25,371 --> 00:02:27,000
Plain, think step by step, helps a little.

55
00:02:27,000 --> 00:02:30,065
Better is to spell out the steps:

56
00:02:30,065 --> 00:02:32,945
first do this, then that,
then the next thing.

57
00:02:32,945 --> 00:02:34,760
Best for review is structure.

58
00:02:34,760 --> 00:02:39,767
Ask Claude to put its reasoning inside
thinking tags and the final result inside

59
00:02:39,767 --> 00:02:40,518
answer tags.

60
00:02:40,518 --> 00:02:45,338
Now you can read the work, and your code or
a colleague can pull out just the

61
00:02:45,338 --> 00:02:45,776
answer.

62
00:02:46,376 --> 00:02:51,287
For bigger projects, run each step as its own
prompt and pass the output forward.

63
00:02:51,287 --> 00:02:53,105
That's called prompt chaining.

64
00:02:53,105 --> 00:02:58,198
Between steps you can check the result, fix a
mistake early, or rerun just one step.

65
00:02:58,198 --> 00:03:03,169
If step two found the wrong trends, you fix
step two, not the whole strategy deck.

66
00:03:03,769 --> 00:03:04,328
Takeaway:

67
00:03:04,328 --> 00:03:09,232
make the reasoning visible, and check each
output before the next step uses it.

68
00:03:09,832 --> 00:03:10,886
Chapter three.

69
00:03:10,886 --> 00:03:12,543
Decompose it yourself.

70
00:03:13,143 --> 00:03:14,399
Pause the video now.

71
00:03:14,399 --> 00:03:15,340
Take this task:

72
00:03:15,340 --> 00:03:18,228
plan the launch of a new
product next quarter.

73
00:03:18,228 --> 00:03:19,986
Write four to six sub-tasks.

74
00:03:19,986 --> 00:03:25,197
For each one, note its input, its output, and
how you'd check that output is right.

75
00:03:25,197 --> 00:03:29,781
Then write the prompt for step one, using
explicit step-by-step phrasing.

76
00:03:29,781 --> 00:03:32,543
Give yourself five minutes,
then press play.

77
00:03:33,143 --> 00:03:34,780
Here's one possible answer.

78
00:03:34,780 --> 00:03:37,206
Define the audience and the launch goal.

79
00:03:37,206 --> 00:03:38,480
Scan the competitors.

80
00:03:38,480 --> 00:03:41,027
Write the positioning and the key message.

81
00:03:41,027 --> 00:03:43,271
Choose channels and split the budget.

82
00:03:43,271 --> 00:03:45,879
Then build the timeline
and list the risks.

83
00:03:45,879 --> 00:03:48,426
Yours may look different, and that's fine.

84
00:03:49,025 --> 00:03:49,663
Takeaway:

85
00:03:49,663 --> 00:03:53,421
every step gets one job, and an
output you can check.

86
00:03:54,021 --> 00:03:55,030
Chapter four.

87
00:03:55,030 --> 00:03:56,582
Know when to use it.

88
00:03:57,183 --> 00:04:02,273
Decompose when the task has several stages,
involves numbers, drives an important

89
00:04:02,273 --> 00:04:05,729
decision, or when someone needs
to audit the reasoning.

90
00:04:05,729 --> 00:04:10,944
Keep it to one prompt for simple lookups and
quick rewrites, where extra steps only

91
00:04:10,944 --> 00:04:12,075
add time and cost.

92
00:04:12,675 --> 00:04:13,303
Takeaway:

93
00:04:13,303 --> 00:04:17,137
decompose when the work has
stages, numbers, or stakes.

94
00:04:17,137 --> 00:04:18,950
Otherwise, keep it simple.

95
00:04:19,550 --> 00:04:20,456
Let's recap.

96
00:04:20,456 --> 00:04:22,495
Big prompts hide many jobs.

97
00:04:23,095 --> 00:04:26,551
Chain of thought and explicit
phrasing make the steps visible.

98
00:04:27,151 --> 00:04:31,311
Each step gets one job, a clear input,
and an output you can check.

99
00:04:31,911 --> 00:04:36,284
And you decompose when the work is
multi-stage, numeric, or high-stakes.

100
00:04:36,884 --> 00:04:38,051
Your practice task:

101
00:04:38,051 --> 00:04:42,905
this week, take one real request you'd
normally send Claude as a single prompt.

102
00:04:42,905 --> 00:04:47,942
Run it once as it is, then again as a chain of
steps with the reasoning shown, and

103
00:04:47,942 --> 00:04:49,171
compare the answers.

104
00:04:49,171 --> 00:04:52,980
Post your step list, and what
changed, in the Skool community.

105
00:04:52,980 --> 00:04:54,639
See you in the next lesson.
